  "id": "learn-to-play-lancer",
  "title": "Learn to Play Lancer",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "compatibility": {
    "minimum": "13.351",
    "verified": "13.351",
    "maximum": "13.351"
  },
  "description": "<p>I've created this short, simple, two-part mission to help teach new GMs how to run Lancer RPG on Foundry and teach new players how to play Lancer. I've kept the theme for this mission intentionally vague, so folks who want to just run two combat scenes can do so, but groups that wish to dip their toes into Lancer's amazing setting and lore can use this as a staging point for further action.</p>
<p>At its core, this mission involves escorting a cargo truck through territory that is beset by enemies - be they pirates, raiders, bandits, or whatever else. After an encounter with these enemies, the players are charged with defending a lightly fortified base from enemy attack. This can also be interpreted as holding off attackers while the fort's residents can safely get underground, holding a dropship landing pad until the Lancers can extract, or guarding a planted bomb while it arms. How you make this mission yours is up to you, but I recommend leaning into your players' interests and intuitions - let them lead the way!</p>
<hr>
<p>There are two combat encounters in this mission: an Escort scene "Bring it On Home," and a Holdout scene "Every Good Fort has a Gap." Maps for these scenes can be found in the Scenes tab, pregenerated player characters (Pilots, Mechs, and any Deployables) and enemies encountered in each scene can be found in the Actors tab, and Journal guides for each scene can be found in the Journal tab.</p>
<p>For each combat, I've made a Journal entry with instructions how how to start the scene, the objectives and success/failure criteria of the Sitrep, which enemies start on the field, and how to deploy additional forces as the scene unfolds. You can drag-and-drop enemies from these Journals onto the map, or you can drag them from each combat's folder in the Actors tab. I have labeled each NPC with deployment criteria in parentheses: the first number notes the player count needed for that NPC to start deployed, and the number after "R" notes the player count necessary to add that NPC to the reinforcement pool. Some enemies have different versions for different player counts!</p>
<p>This mission can be run for 3-5 players. If you have fewer players, I recommend each player control multiple characters; if you have more, I recommend running two separate groups. I have six pre-made Pilots ready-to-go, each showcasing a different playstyle and using different configurations of the first-party starter mechs: the Everest, Sagarmatha, and Chomolungma. The three Everest builds (Fafnir, Catch, Djinn) are the easiest to pick up, and each functions primarily as a damage-dealer. The Sagarmatha (Marshmallow) functions like a 'tank' or leader, and the two Chomolungma mechs (Jetstream, Lodestar) play more like supportive or controlling 'mages' through the use of hacking, spotting, and status conditions.</p>
",
